        <nav class="breadcrumb reveal"><a href="index.html">Home</a> / <a href="kenya.html">Kenya</a> / Samburu</nav>
        <p class="article__lead reveal">North of the equator, where the savanna gives way to red earth and doum palms, Samburu feels wilder and more remote than Kenya's southern parks — home to species you'll see nowhere else, and to the Samburu, warm cousins of the Maasai.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Why visit Samburu?</h2>
        <p class="reveal">Samburu National Reserve, on the banks of the Ewaso Ng'iro river in Kenya's arid north, offers dramatic scenery, excellent predator sightings and a real sense of frontier wilderness — with far fewer vehicles than the Mara.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">The Samburu Special Five</h2>
        <p class="reveal">Samburu is famous for five dry-country species found here and rarely elsewhere: the <strong>reticulated giraffe, Grevy's zebra, Beisa oryx, gerenuk</strong> (the "giraffe-necked" antelope) and <strong>Somali ostrich</strong> — alongside elephant, lion, leopard and cheetah.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">The Samburu people</h2>
        <p class="reveal">The Samburu are a semi-nomadic people known for their striking red dress and beadwork — the same beadwork that inspired our brand. We arrange respectful, women-led visits and meetings with Samburu women's cooperatives, so your visit gives back.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Best time to visit</h2>
        <p class="reveal">The dry seasons (<strong>June–October, December–March</strong>) concentrate wildlife along the river and offer the best game viewing. See our <a href="best-time-kenya.html">best time to visit Kenya</a> guide.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Where to stay</h2>
        <p class="reveal">We favour intimate riverside camps and conservancy lodges with female guides, walking safaris and genuine community ties.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">How to get there</h2>
        <p class="reveal">A short charter flight from Nairobi, or a scenic drive past Mount Kenya. Samburu pairs wonderfully with the Mara and Laikipia for a wilder, off-the-beaten-track journey.</p>

